msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of protection from fire"
msgid ""
"“And these three men, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, fell down bound into the\n"
" midst of the burning fiery furnace.\n"
"\n"
" Then Nebuchadnezzar the king was astonished, and rose up in haste, and spake,\n"
" and said unto his counsellors, Did not we cast three men bound into the midst\n"
" of the fire? They answered and said unto the king, True, O king.\n"
"\n"
" He answered and said, Lo, I see four men loose, walking in the midst of the\n"
" fire, and they have no hurt; and the form of the fourth is like the Son of God.\n"
"\n"
" Then Nebuchadnezzar came near to the mouth of the burning fiery furnace, and\n"
" spake, and said, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, ye servants of the most high\n"
" God, come forth, and come hither. Then Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, came\n"
" forth of the midst of the fire.\n"
"\n"
" And the princes, governors, and captains, and the king’s counsellors, being\n"
" gathered together, saw these men, upon whose bodies the fire had no power, nor\n"
" was an hair of their head singed, neither were their coats changed, nor the\n"
" smell of fire had passed on them.”\n"
"    -KJV Bible, Daniel 3:23-27.\n"
msgstr ""
"“沙得拉、米煞和亚伯尼歌三人，被捆着落入烈火窑。\n"
"\n"
"尼布甲尼撒王惊讶，急忙起身问谋士：我们不是捆住三个人扔进火里吗？他们回答：是，王啊。\n"
"\n"
"王说：看，我见四个人松了绑，在火中行走，毫发无伤；第四位的样子像神之子。\n"
"\n"
"王走近窑口，喊：至高神的仆人沙得拉、米煞、亚伯尼歌，出来，到这里来！三人便从火中出来。\n"
"\n"
"众首领、官员、军长和谋士聚来，看见火不能伤他们，头发未焦，衣服未变，连火的气味也没有。”\n"
"    -KJV Bible, Daniel 3:23-27.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of resist corrosion"
msgid "<ring>\n"
msgstr "<ring>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of see invisible"
msgid ""
"“Here is my secret. It is very simple: It is only with the heart that one can\n"
" see rightly; what is essential is invisible to the eye.”\n"
"    -Antoine de Saint Exupéry, _The Little Prince_. 1943.\n"
msgstr ""
"“这是我的秘密，很简单：只有用心，才能看清。真正重要的东西，眼睛看不见。”\n"
"    -Antoine de Saint Exupéry, _The Little Prince_. 1943.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of slaying"
msgid ""
"“Life is too short to occupy oneself with the slaying of the slain more than\n"
" once.”\n"
"    -Thomas Huxley\n"
msgstr ""
"“人生太短，不值得把已被杀死的东西再杀一遍。”\n"
"    -Thomas Huxley\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of stealth"
msgid ""
"“Then as part of the game\n"
" She completely forgot where she'd hidden herself\n"
" And she spent the rest of her time\n"
" Trying to find the parts”\n"
"    -Howard Jones, “Hide and Seek”. 1984.\n"
msgstr ""
"“作为游戏的一部分，\n"
"她完全忘了自己藏在哪里，\n"
"余下时光，\n"
"都在寻找那些碎片。”\n"
"    -Howard Jones, “Hide and Seek”. 1984.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of strength"
msgid ""
"“That which does not kill us makes us stronger.”\n"
"    -Friedrich Nietzsche\n"
msgstr ""
"“凡杀不死我们的，都使我们更强。”\n"
"    -Friedrich Nietzsche\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of willpower"
msgid ""
"“I am, indeed, a king, because I know how to rule myself.”\n"
"    -Pietro Aretino, letter to Mr. Agostino Ricchi, May 10th, 1537. from\n"
"      _The Works of Aretino, Translated into English from the original Italian_,\n"
"      trans. Samuel Putnam. 1926\n"
msgstr ""
"“我的确是王，因为我知道如何统治自己。”\n"
"    -Pietro Aretino, letter to Mr. Agostino Ricchi, May 10th, 1537. from\n"
"      _The Works of Aretino, Translated into English from the original Italian_,\n"
"      trans. Samuel Putnam. 1926\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:ring of wizardry"
msgid "<ring>\n"
msgstr "<ring>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:robe"
msgid ""
"“CLEOPATRA: Give me my robe, put on my crown; I have\n"
" Immortal longings in me”\n"
"    -William Shakespeare, _Anthony & Cleopatra_, V, ii. ca. 1605.\n"
msgstr ""
"“克娄巴特拉：给我长袍，戴上王冠；\n"
"我心中怀着不朽的渴望。”\n"
"    -William Shakespeare, _Anthony & Cleopatra_, V, ii. ca. 1605.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scale mail"
msgid "<leather armour>\n"
msgstr "<leather armour>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scimitar"
msgid ""
"“The museum-cabinet and huge library arrogated to themselves the entire lower\n"
" floor — there were the controversial and incompatible books that are somehow\n"
" the history of the nineteenth century; there were scimitars from Nishapur, in\n"
" whose frozen crescents the wind and violence of battle seemed to be living\n"
" on.”\n"
"    -Jorge Luis Borges, _The Form of the Sword_. 1953.\n"
"     trans. Andrew Hurley.\n"
msgstr ""
"“陈列室和庞大图书馆占据整座底层。那里有争论不休、互不相容，却在某种意义上构成十九世纪历史的书；还有来自尼沙普尔的弯刀，在凝固的新月里，战场的风与暴力仿佛仍然活着。”\n"
"    -Jorge Luis Borges, _The Form of the Sword_. 1953.\n"
"     trans. Andrew Hurley.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll"
msgid ""
"“To drift with every passion till my soul\n"
" Is a stringed lute on which all winds can play,\n"
" Is it for this that I have given away\n"
" Mine ancient wisdom, and austere control?\n"
" Methinks my life is a twice-written scroll\n"
" Scrawled over on some boyish holiday.”\n"
"    -Oscar Wilde, _Helas_. 1881.\n"
msgstr ""
"“随每种激情漂流，直到灵魂\n"
"变成任所有风弹奏的琵琶，\n"
"难道为此，我才放弃\n"
"古老智慧与严苛自制？\n"
"我想，我的生命如二度书写的卷轴，\n"
"在少年假日里被胡乱涂满。”\n"
"    -Oscar Wilde, _Helas_. 1881.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of acquirement"
msgid ""
"He had stopped trying to think. He just repeated his litany over and over:\n"
"“I am an animal, you see that. I don’t have the words, they didn’t teach\n"
"me the words. I don’t know how to think, the bastards didn’t let me learn\n"
"how to think. But if you really are... all-powerful... all-knowing... then you\n"
"figure it out! Look into my heart. I know that everything you need is in there.\n"
"It has to be. I never sold my soul to anyone! It’s mine, it’s human! You\n"
"take from me what it is I want... it just can’t be that I would want something\n"
"bad! Damn it all, I can’t think of anything, except those words of his...\n"
"‘HAPPINESS FOR EVERYBODY, FREE, AND NO ONE WILL GO AWAY UNSATISFIED!’”\n"
"    -Arkady and Boris Strugatsky, “Roadside Picnic”. 1972.\n"
"    trans. Antonina W. Bouis, 1977.\n"
msgstr ""
"他已不再试着思考，只一遍遍重复祷词：\n"
"“你看，我是畜生。我没有词语，他们没教我词语。我不知道怎样思考，那些混蛋不准我学。但如果你真是……全能……全知……你来弄明白！看看我的心，我知道你需要的一切都在那里，必须在那里。我没把灵魂卖给任何人！它是我的，是人的！你从我这里取出我想要的……我不可能想要坏东西！该死，我什么都想不出，只剩他的那句话……\n"
"‘让人人幸福，免费，而且谁也不会不满意地离开！’”\n"
"    -Arkady and Boris Strugatsky, “Roadside Picnic”. 1972.\n"
"    trans. Antonina W. Bouis, 1977.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of amnesia"
msgid ""
"“But revenge is hollow. I'd prefer amnesia.”\n"
"    -Tera Lynn Childs\n"
msgstr ""
"“复仇空洞。我宁愿失忆。”\n"
"    -Tera Lynn Childs\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of blinking"
msgid "<scroll>\n"
msgstr "<scroll>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of brand weapon"
msgid "<scroll>\n"
msgstr "<scroll>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of butterflies"
msgid ""
"“It used to be thought that the events that changed the world were things like\n"
" big bombs, maniac politicians, huge earthquakes, or vast population movements,\n"
" but it has now been realized that this is a very old-fashioned view held by\n"
" people totally out of touch with modern thought. The things that really change\n"
" the world, according to Chaos theory, are the tiny things. A butterfly flaps\n"
" its wings in the Amazonian jungle, and subsequently a storm ravages half of\n"
" Europe...”\n"
"    -Neil Gaiman and Terry Pratchett, _Good Omens: The Nice And Accurate\n"
"     Prophecies of Agnes Nutter, Witch_. 1990.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""
"“过去，人们以为改变世界的是大炸弹、疯狂政客、大地震或庞大人口迁移；如今才知道，这是完全脱离现代思想者的老派看法。按混沌理论，真正改变世界的是微小之事。一只蝴蝶在亚马孙丛林扇翼，随后，一场风暴蹂躏半个欧洲……”\n"
"    -Neil Gaiman and Terry Pratchett, _Good Omens: The Nice And Accurate\n"
"     Prophecies of Agnes Nutter, Witch_. 1990.\n"
"\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of enchant armour"
msgid "<scroll>\n"
msgstr "<scroll>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of enchant weapon"
msgid "<scroll>\n"
msgstr "<scroll>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of fear"
msgid ""
"“I will revenge my injuries: if I cannot inspire love, I will cause fear.”\n"
"    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus_,\n"
"     Vol. II, Chapter 9. 1818 (1st Ed.)\n"
msgstr ""
"“我要为所受伤害复仇：既然不能唤起爱，我便制造恐惧。”\n"
"    -Mary Shelley, _Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus_,\n"
"     Vol. II, Chapter 9. 1818 (1st Ed.)\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of fog"
msgid ""
"“Calvin: I used to hate writing assignments, but now I enjoy them. I realized\n"
"         that the purpose of writing is to inflate weak ideas, obscure poor\n"
"         reasoning, and inhibit clarity. With a little practice, writing can be\n"
"         an intimidating and impenetrable fog!”\n"
"    -Bill Watterson, _Calvin and Hobbes_. February 11th, 1993.\n"
msgstr ""
"“卡尔文：我以前讨厌作文，现在很喜欢。我明白了，写作的目的就是膨胀薄弱观点、掩盖糟糕推理、阻止清晰表达。稍加练习，文章就能变成令人畏惧、穿不透的浓雾！”\n"
"    -Bill Watterson, _Calvin and Hobbes_. February 11th, 1993.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of identify"
msgid ""
"“This, what is it in itself, and by itself, according to its proper\n"
" constitution? What is the substance of it? What is the matter, or\n"
" proper use? What is the form or efficient cause? What is it for in\n"
" this world, and how long will it abide? Thus must thou examine all\n"
" things, that present themselves unto thee.”\n"
"    -Marcus Aurelius, _Marcus Aurelius Antoninus - His Meditations concerning\n"
"     himselfe_, Book VIII, X. trans. Meric Casaubon, 1634.\n"
msgstr ""
"“这东西依其本性，自己究竟是什么？实质是什么？材料或正当用途是什么？形式或直接原因是什么？在世上为何而存在，又将持续多久？面对一切事物，你都应如此审察。”\n"
"    -Marcus Aurelius, _Marcus Aurelius Antoninus - His Meditations concerning\n"
"     himselfe_, Book VIII, X. trans. Meric Casaubon, 1634.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of immolation"
msgid "<scroll>\n"
msgstr "<scroll>\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of revelation"
msgid ""
"“‘What a useful thing a pocket-map is!’ I remarked.\n"
" ‘That’s another thing we’ve learned from your Nation,’ said Mein Herr,\n"
"    ‘map-making. But we’ve carried it much further than you. What do you\n"
"    consider the largest map that would be really useful?’\n"
" ‘About six inches to the mile.’\n"
" ‘Only six inches!’ exclaimed Mein Herr. ‘We very soon got to six yards to the\n"
"     mile. Then we tried a hundred yards to the mile. And then came the grandest\n"
"     idea of all! We actually made a map of the country, on the scale of a mile\n"
"     to the mile!’\n"
" ‘Have you used it much?’ I enquired.\n"
" ‘It has never been spread out, yet,’ said Mein Herr: ‘the farmers objected:\n"
"     they said it would cover the whole country, and shut out the sunlight! So\n"
"     we now use the country itself, as its own map, and I assure you it does\n"
"     nearly as well.’”\n"
"    -Lewis Carroll, _Sylvie and Bruno Concluded_, Chapter XI. 1895\n"
msgstr ""
"“口袋地图真有用！”我说。\n"
"“那也是我们向贵国学的，”先生说，“制图。但我们走得更远。你觉得最大多大的地图才实用？”\n"
"“一英里画六英寸左右。”\n"
"“才六英寸！我们很快便画到一英里六码，后来试一百码。再后来有了最伟大的主意：一英里画一英里，给全国做了地图！”\n"
"“常用吗？”\n"
"“还从未展开。农民反对，说会盖住全国，挡掉阳光！所以现在我们用国土本身作地图，我保证，效果几乎一样好。”\n"
"    -Lewis Carroll, _Sylvie and Bruno Concluded_, Chapter XI. 1895\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of noise"
msgid ""
"“The nature of the place where the Library was founded—a sanatorium rumored to\n"
" harbor the pitiably deformed—had proven a lure to people with no desire at all\n"
" for ‘regular human communication.’ I remember the case of a man who was normal\n"
" in every respect, in words, in reasoning, in the practice of his profession,\n"
" but strayed from this ‘normalcy’ in only one way: his inexplicable need to fill\n"
" thousands of sheets of foolscap paper with seemingly meaningless words,\n"
" phonemes close to wailing sounds, cries of fury and pain in relentless\n"
" successions, fragments of sentences and pleas addressed to God-knows-whom.”\n"
"    -Giorgio de Maria, _The Twenty Days of Turin_, 1975.\n"
"     trans. Ramon Glazov, 2016\n"
msgstr ""
"“图书馆建立的地点，原是一座据说收留可怜畸形者的疗养院，这吸引了毫不渴望‘正常人际交流’的人。我记得一个人，言谈、推理、执业处处正常，只有一点例外：莫名需要在数千张大纸上写满似乎无意义的词、近似哀号的音节、连续的愤怒与痛苦叫喊、残句，以及不知向谁发出的恳求。”\n"
"    -Giorgio de Maria, _The Twenty Days of Turin_, 1975.\n"
"     trans. Ramon Glazov, 2016\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of silence"
msgid ""
"“And in the naked light I saw\n"
"   Ten thousand people, maybe more.\n"
" People talking without speaking,\n"
"   People hearing without listening;\n"
" People writing songs\n"
"   That voices never shared:\n"
" No one dared\n"
"   Disturb the sound\n"
"   Of silence.”\n"
"    -Simon & Garfunkel, _The Sound of Silence_. 1964.\n"
msgstr ""
"“在裸露灯光中，我看见\n"
"一万人，也许更多。\n"
"人们交谈却不说话，\n"
"人们听见却不聆听；\n"
"人们写下歌曲，\n"
"却从未有人歌唱。\n"
"谁也不敢\n"
"扰乱那\n"
"寂静之声。”\n"
"    -Simon & Garfunkel, _The Sound of Silence_. 1964.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of summoning"
msgid ""
"“When thou attended gloriously from heaven, Shalt in the sky appear, and from\n"
" thee send Thy summoning archangels to proclaim Thy dread tribunal.”\n"
"    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book III, 1667.\n"
msgstr ""
"“你从天上荣耀地降临，显现在空中，从身边遣出召集众生的大天使，宣告你可畏的审判台。”\n"
"    -John Milton, _Paradise Lost_, Book III, 1667.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scroll of teleportation"
msgid ""
"“Eiris sazun idisi\n"
" sazun hera duoder\n"
" suma hapt heptidun\n"
" suma heri lezidun\n"
" suma clubodun\n"
" umbi cuoniouuidi\n"
" insprinc haptbandun\n"
" inuar uigandun\n"
"\n"
" [Once sat women,\n"
" they sat here then there.\n"
" some fastened bonds,\n"
" some impeded an army,\n"
" some unraveled fetters:\n"
" Escape the bonds,\n"
" flee the enemy!]”\n"
"    -First Merseburg incantation, trans. Patricia Giangrosso, 2001\n"
msgstr ""
"“Eiris sazun idisi\n"
"sazun hera duoder\n"
"suma hapt heptidun\n"
"suma heri lezidun\n"
"suma clubodun\n"
"umbi cuoniouuidi\n"
"insprinc haptbandun\n"
"inuar uigandun\n"
"\n"
"［妇人曾坐在那里，\n"
"她们坐在此处、彼处。\n"
"有的系紧束缚，\n"
"有的阻住军队，\n"
"有的解开锁链：\n"
"挣脱束缚，\n"
"逃离敌人！］”\n"
"    -First Merseburg incantation, trans. Patricia Giangrosso, 2001\n"

