msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:scythe"
msgid ""
"“It was instinct. Illogical as lightning striking and not hurting. Each day the\n"
" grain must be cut. It had to be cut. Why? Well, it just did, that was all. He\n"
" laughed at the scythe in his big hands. Then, whistling, he took it out to the\n"
" ripe and waiting field and did the work. He thought himself a little mad.\n"
" Hell, it was an ordinary-enough wheat field, really, wasn't it?”\n"
"    -Ray Bradbury, _The Scythe_. 1943.\n"
msgstr ""
"“那是本能，像闪电击中却不伤人一样不合逻辑。每天必须收割谷物。就是得割。为什么？没有为什么。他望着大手中的镰刀发笑，吹着口哨走向成熟、等候的田地，开始干活。他觉得自己有些疯。可那不过是一块再普通不过的麦田，真的，不是吗？”\n"
"    -Ray Bradbury, _The Scythe_. 1943.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shillelagh \"devastator\""
msgid ""
"“‘Hurrah! my boys,’ says I, my shillelagh I let fly.\n"
" Some Galway boys were by, they saw I was a hobbling;\n"
" Then with a loud ‘hurrah!’ they joined me in the fray.\n"
" Faugh-a-ballagh! Clear the way! for the rocky road to Dublin!”\n"
"\n"
"    -D.K. Gavan, _Rocky Road to Dublin_. 19th Century.\n"
msgstr ""
"“‘万岁，伙计们！’我说，挥出了木棒。\n"
"旁边几个戈尔韦人见我跛行，\n"
"大喊万岁，加入混战。\n"
"让路！清路！为通往都柏林的崎岖道路！”\n"
"    -D.K. Gavan, _Rocky Road to Dublin_. 19th Century.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:shortbow"
msgid ""
"“You are the bows from which your children as living arrows are sent forth.”\n"
"    -Khalil Ghibran, _The Prophet_, “On Children”. 1923.\n"
msgstr ""
"“你们是弓，儿女是从你们身上射出的活箭。”\n"
"    -Khalil Ghibran, _The Prophet_, “On Children”. 1923.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:short sword"
msgid ""
"“Who was the first that forged the deadly blade?\n"
" Of rugged steel his savage soul was made”\n"
"    -Albius Tibullus, _Elegies_ I, xi. ca. 25 B.C.\n"
"     trans. James Grainger, 1822.\n"
msgstr ""
"“最初是谁锻造致命刀刃？\n"
"他野蛮的灵魂，必由粗钢铸成。”\n"
"    -Albius Tibullus, _Elegies_ I, xi. ca. 25 B.C.\n"
"     trans. James Grainger, 1822.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:sling"
msgid ""
"“And David put his hand in his bag, and took thence a stone, and slang it, and\n"
" smote the Philistine in his forehead, that the stone sunk into his forehead;\n"
" and he fell upon his face to the earth.”\n"
"    -KJV Bible, 1 Samuel 17:49.\n"
msgstr ""
"“大卫伸手入袋，取石甩出，打中非利士人的额。石头陷入额中，那人便扑面倒地。”\n"
"    -KJV Bible, 1 Samuel 17:49.\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:greatsling \"punk\""
msgid ""
"“Thrice whirling round his head\n"
" The whistling thong, Mezentius took his aim.\n"
" Clean through his temples hissed the molten lead,\n"
" And prostrate in the dust, the gallant youth lay dead.”\n"
"    -Virgil, Aeneid 9\n"
"     trans. E. Fairfax Taylor\n"
msgstr ""
"“梅曾提乌斯将呼啸的投石索\n"
"绕头挥转三次，瞄准。\n"
"熔铅嘶鸣着穿透太阳穴，\n"
"勇敢青年倒毙尘土。”\n"
"    -Virgil, Aeneid 9\n"
"     trans. E. Fairfax Taylor\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:giant club \"skullcrusher\""
msgid ""
"“Or burst the vanish’d Hero’s lofty mound;\n"
" Far on the solitary shore he sleeps:\n"
" He fell, and falling nations mourn’d around;\n"
" But now not one of saddening thousands weeps,\n"
" Nor warlike worshipper his vigil keeps\n"
" Where demi-gods appear’d, as records tell.\n"
" Remove yon skull from out the scatter’d heaps:\n"
" Is that a temple where a God may dwell?\n"
" Why ev’n the worm at last disdains her shatter’d cell!\n"
"\n"
" Look on its broken arch, its ruin’d wall,\n"
" Its chambers desolate, and portals foul:\n"
" Yes, this was once Ambition’s airy hall,\n"
" The dome of Thought, the palace of the Soul:\n"
" Behold through each lack-lustre, eyeless hole,\n"
" The gay recess of Wisdom and of Wit\n"
" And Passion’s host, that never brook’d control:\n"
" Can all saint, sage, or sophist ever writ,\n"
" People this lonely tower, this tenement refit?”\n"
"    -Lord Byron, _Childe Harold's Pilgrimage_, Canto the Second, Stanzas V-VI\n"
msgstr ""
"“掘开逝去英雄高耸的坟冢，\n"
"他长眠在孤独海岸远方。\n"
"他倒下时，诸国哀悼，\n"
"如今成千上万悲伤者无一再哭，\n"
"也无尚武崇拜者守夜。\n"
"史书说，半神曾现于此。\n"
"从散乱骨堆取出那颗头骨：\n"
"那是可让神居住的殿堂吗？\n"
"如今连蛆虫也嫌弃这破屋！\n"
"\n"
"看破拱、毁墙、空室与污门。\n"
"这里曾是野心的高堂，\n"
"思想的圆顶，灵魂的宫殿。\n"
"透过无光无眼的洞，\n"
"看见智慧、机敏的欢乐居所，\n"
"和从不服管束的激情。\n"
"圣徒、贤者与智辩者所有著述，\n"
"能重新住满孤塔、修复这间居所吗？”\n"
"    -Lord Byron, _Childe Harold's Pilgrimage_, Canto the Second, Stanzas V-VI\n"

msgctxt "textdb:descript/quotes.txt:spear"
msgid ""
"“The halberd is inferior to the spear on the battlefield. With the spear you\n"
" can take the initiative; the halberd is defensive. In the hands of one of two\n"
" men of equal ability, the spear gives a little extra strength.”\n"
"    -Miyamoto Musashi, _The Book of Five Rings_. 1645.\n"
msgstr ""
"“战场上，戟不如枪。枪可抢先，戟则偏于防守。两人本领相同，持枪者会略占优势。”\n"
"    -Miyamoto Musashi, _The Book of Five Rings_. 1645.\n"

